title: mf repo pull-config
tags: [[CLI command]] Configuration Sync
kind: reference
summary: Merge another repository's configuration into this one's

`mf repo pull-config <path> <from>` merges the `main` branch of `<from>`'s
`.metafolder/config/` into `<path>`'s ([[Repository configuration]]), the way
[[mf repo update-config]] merges the defaults: what `<from>` changed joins `<path>`'s
own edits. It is how two repositories get the identical schemas a sync requires
([[Why schemas must be identical]]). Each path names a `.metafolder/` directory or the
root holding one; no daemon is involved, and a loaded `<path>` reads its new schema at
`mf schema reload`.

* `<from>` is only read. Its edits must be committed — what its daemon reads is its
  working tree, and only commits are fetched —, or the command refuses and says how.
* `<path>`'s uncommitted edits are committed first, as for `update-config`.
* Prints `configuration merged from <from>` or `configuration already up to date`.
* A conflict leaves `<path>`'s `config/` as it was, lists the files (full paths) on
  stderr with the git commands that finish the merge by hand, and exits 1. What was
  fetched stays on the branch `pulled` of `<path>`'s `config/`, for that merge.

!! The first pull

Each repository's `config/` started with a commit of its own, so two of them share no
commit until a first pull joins their histories (the merge commit has both as
parents; later pulls merge from it as git would). That first merge takes as its base
the latest defaults both `default` branches hold — the tree of
`~/.config/metafolder/daemon/repo-default/` they were both installed or updated from —,
and an empty base when there is none, every file that differs then a conflict. A merge
by hand after a conflict on a first pull has only git's own empty base
(`git merge --allow-unrelated-histories pulled`, as the help says), so it may list more
than the command did.
